# No useless comments

Only write a comment when it explains something the code cannot express on its own.

## Do not write
- Comments that restate the code (`i++ // increment i`, `// set the color`).
- Comments that label obvious structure (`// imports`, `// helper functions`, `// return`).
- Redundant docstrings that repeat the function name/signature.
- Commented-out code, `// removed X`, or backwards-compat breadcrumbs — just delete it.
- End-of-line notes that duplicate a self-evident value or name.

## Do write (sparingly)
- The *why* behind non-obvious logic: constraints, edge cases, tradeoffs, workarounds.
- Units, coordinate systems, or invariants that aren't clear from the code.
- Links to a spec/issue when it clarifies intent.

Prefer clear names and small functions over explanatory comments. If a comment is
needed to understand *what* the code does, consider rewriting the code instead.
